const sites={"jezero":{"title":"Jezero Crater","subtitle":"Ancient lake basin, river delta, sedimentary archive, and Perseverance investigation","basis":"Observation basis. This is a remote field note compiled from NASA spacecraft/rover observations, archived mission data, and peer-reviewed planetary-science literature. It does not represent a physical field visit.","meta":[{"label":"SITE","value":"Jezero Crater, Nili Fossae region"},{"label":"APPROX. CENTER","value":"18.4° N, 77.6° E"},{"label":"SCALE","value":"~45 km crater diameter"},{"label":"PRIMARY MISSION","value":"Mars 2020 Perseverance (landed 18 Feb 2021)"}],"sections":[{"title":"1. Site identification and geographical setting","items":[{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Jezero is an impact crater about 45 km (28 mi) across on the western margin of the Isidis impact basin, within the mineralogically diverse Nili Fossae region. Its floor lies inside a broader landscape of ancient crust, fractured terrain, and olivine- and carbonate-bearing units. The crater is distinguished by a preserved western fan-shaped deposit and an outlet valley on the eastern rim. [1] [2]"},{"type":"p","text":"Scientific interpretation. The geomorphic arrangement is that of an ancient open-basin lake: valleys entered the crater from the west and north, sediment accumulated where water entered the basin, and drainage eventually passed through the eastern outlet. This geometry is a major reason Jezero was selected as a landing site for an astrobiology-focused rover. [2]"},{"type":"p","text":"The coordinates above are a practical site-center value for mapping the crater as a whole; individual Perseverance observations are tied to much more precise rover-local coordinates and named geologic units. For an interactive website, crater-wide orbital layers and rover-scale observations should therefore be treated as different spatial scales rather than as interchangeable datasets."}]},{"title":"2. Field observations from imagery and rover records","items":[{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Orbital images show a lobate western delta or fan protruding into the crater. From the landing site, Mastcam-Z resolved the remnant as a raised body of layered sedimentary rock. As Perseverance approached and entered the delta, rover images revealed cliffs, beds, cross-stratified intervals, fine-grained sedimentary units, resistant ledges, and boulder-bearing packages. [3] [10]"},{"type":"p","text":"Scientific interpretation. The basal and middle sedimentary packages record deposition by flowing water and standing water, while the upper boulder-bearing deposits require substantially higher transport energy. The rover-scale stratigraphy therefore indicates that Jezero was not simply a quiet lake with a single depositional episode; water levels, sediment supply, and flow energy changed through time. [3] [6] [7]"},{"type":"p","text":"Mission record. Before reaching the delta, Perseverance investigated the Máaz and Séítah crater-floor units. The unexpected result was that important parts of the floor are igneous rather than a simple stack of lake sediment. Samples and contact-science observations show that these igneous rocks were later altered by water, preserving both an igneous history and an aqueous overprint. [1] [4] [5]"},{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Within the western fan, Perseverance examined outcrops including Skinner Ridge and Wildcat Ridge and collected drilled cores from sedimentary rocks. The Shenandoah formation studied along the eastern scarp of the fan comprises tens of meters of sedimentary strata, including laminated sandstones and finer-grained intervals, and was sampled repeatedly during the delta campaign. [6] [7]"},{"type":"p","text":"Aeolian modification is superposed on the ancient record. Modern wind has eroded exposed rock faces, concentrated loose sand into ripples and dunes, and removed or mantled some primary textures. Field interpretation therefore requires separating depositional structures from younger erosional forms. [7]"}]},{"title":"3. Geological interpretation and history","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"Impact excavation formed the Jezero basin in ancient Martian crust. The impact predates the lake and delta record preserved inside the crater. [2]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Olivine-rich and carbonate-bearing materials were emplaced or altered within the broader Nili Fossae–Jezero region before and/or during later erosional reworking; their exact origin remains debated. [2]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"A river–lake system later occupied the crater. Water entered through inlet valleys, deposited a fan/delta, and at times maintained standing water capable of accumulating fine sediment. [2] [3] [6]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Perseverance stratigraphy supports a sequence that includes fluvial, lacustrine, deltaic, and high-energy flood conditions rather than one uniform lake environment. [3] [6] [7]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"After surface water waned, erosion, wind transport, impact gardening, and chemical alteration modified the exposed deposits. Modern conditions are cold, dry, and dominated at the surface by aeolian processes. [7]"},{"type":"p","text":"Interpretive caution. The ordering above is a working geologic history assembled from cross-cutting relations, sedimentary structures, mineral distributions, and rover observations. Absolute ages for many units remain model dependent because crater-count chronology is indirect and the rover has not radiometrically dated the main delta succession in situ."}]},{"title":"4. Mineralogy and composition","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Orbital spectroscopy. CRISM mapped strong olivine, carbonate, and phyllosilicate (clay-mineral) signatures in and around Jezero. Carbonate-bearing material is associated with olivine-rich units, while phyllosilicate-bearing materials occur in watershed and delta-related terrains. Brown and colleagues used CRISM to show that the crater and watershed contain unusually extensive carbonate-bearing exposures for Mars. [2] [9]"},{"type":"p","text":"Rover geochemistry. Perseverance adds millimeter- to centimeter-scale chemistry and mineralogy through PIXL X-ray fluorescence mapping, SHERLOC Raman/fluorescence spectroscopy and imaging, and SuperCam LIBS, VIS/IR, Raman, imaging, and acoustic observations. These instruments test whether orbital spectral units correspond to specific minerals and textures at outcrop scale. [8]"},{"type":"p","text":"Crater-floor composition. Séítah contains coarse-grained, olivine-rich igneous rock interpreted as an olivine cumulate. The floor rocks also record aqueous alteration after crystallization, demonstrating that water interacted with materials that were not themselves deposited as lake sediment. [4] [5]"},{"type":"p","text":"Scientific significance. Carbonates, clays, fine-grained sedimentary rocks, and alteration products are important because they can record water–rock chemistry and may protect organic compounds or textural biosignatures under favorable conditions. Their presence is evidence of potentially habitable environments, not evidence that life existed. [2] [7]"}]},{"title":"5. Water history and environmental conditions","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Established evidence. Jezero contains an inlet–delta–basin–outlet system, deltaic sedimentary structures observed from orbit and the surface, and sedimentary rocks sampled by Perseverance. These observations establish that persistent or repeated liquid-water processes once operated inside the crater. [2] [3] [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"Environmental reconstruction. The lower western-fan succession is consistent with environments ranging from distal river or alluvial settings into standing-water and delta-front settings. Some fine-grained intervals may record low-energy lake conditions, while other strata document gravity flows or channelized transport. Later high-energy floods transported meter-scale boulders into the upper fan. [3] [6] [7]"},{"type":"p","text":"Habitability context. A lake with sediment input, chemical gradients, mineral surfaces, and episodes of water–rock interaction provides several ingredients relevant to microbial habitability. Perseverance therefore prioritizes rocks that both formed or altered in water and have favorable preservation potential. Habitability and biosignature preservation are separate questions; neither by itself demonstrates biology. [7]"}]},{"title":"6. NASA mission observations and sampling","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter: HiRISE and CTX supply regional-to-meter-scale morphology and stereo/topographic context; CRISM supplies visible/near-infrared mineral maps. [2] [9]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Perseverance Mastcam-Z and navigation cameras: stratigraphy, bedding geometry, color, clast distributions, and rover-scale geomorphic context. [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"SuperCam: remote elemental and molecular composition, high-resolution remote imaging, and acoustic measurements. [8]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"PIXL and SHERLOC/WATSON: fine-scale elemental chemistry, Raman/fluorescence mineral–organic context, and close-up texture at abraded surfaces. [8]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"RIMFAX: ground-penetrating radar profiles that add shallow-subsurface structure along the rover traverse. [8]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Sampling system: drilled rock cores have been sealed in sample tubes from crater-floor, delta/fan, and later terrains. These samples preserve material for possible future laboratory investigation; claims about returned-sample results should not be made because the samples have not been returned to Earth. [1] [7]"}]},{"title":"7. Scientific significance","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Jezero joins basin-scale geomorphology, mineralogical diversity, accessible sedimentary stratigraphy, and direct surface measurements in a single field site. The crater preserves a transition from ancient crust and igneous floor materials to aqueous alteration, lake and delta deposition, and later erosional modification. That combination allows scientists to test how Mars moved from locally water-rich conditions toward the cold, arid surface seen today. [2] [4] [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"For astrobiology, the key value is not simply that Jezero once held water. The site contains depositional environments capable of concentrating fine sediment and chemical constituents, minerals associated with aqueous alteration, and cored samples acquired with documented geologic context. Those are the conditions needed for rigorous evaluation of potential biosignature preservation. [6] [7]"}]},{"title":"8. Uncertainties and data limitations","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"The duration, depth, and continuity of Jezero’s lake episodes are not uniquely determined. Different beds may record separate lake stands or hydrologic events rather than one long-lived stable lake. [3] [6]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"The origin and timing of carbonate formation remain debated; orbital spectra identify carbonate-bearing materials but do not by themselves establish whether carbonates formed during alteration, sedimentary reworking, hydrothermal processes, or multiple episodes. [2]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Rover observations are exceptionally detailed but spatially narrow. Perseverance samples a traverse, not the full crater, and inaccessible cliffs or buried units may contain major parts of the record."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Orbital spectroscopy averages mixed materials over larger pixels and can be affected by dust, grain size, viewing geometry, and atmospheric correction; rover measurements sample much smaller targets. [2] [9]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Sedimentary structures constrain transport and depositional environment, but absolute chronology is still largely indirect. A future laboratory analysis of returned samples would be needed for the highest-precision geochronology and many biosignature tests."},{"type":"bullet","text":"No observation cited here establishes past life. Organic molecules or habitable conditions, where identified, require independent lines of evidence before biological origin can be considered."}]},{"title":"9. Relevant NASA data and original resources","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars 2020 PDS mission archive — Mission-wide entry point for Perseverance instrument bundles, documentation, sample dossier, and release notes. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/index.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"PIXL archive — Raw, processed, image, and derived X-ray lithochemistry products. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/pixl.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"SHERLOC archive — Raman/fluorescence spectroscopy plus ACI/WATSON-related archive links. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/sherloc.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"SuperCam archive — LIBS, Raman, VIS/IR, RMI, acoustic, raw and calibrated products. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/supercam.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"RIMFAX archive — Raw/calibrated radar products and browse radargrams. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/rimfax.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MRO CRISM archive — Orbital hyperspectral products for Jezero and the surrounding Nili Fossae region. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/crism.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Perseverance delta image, PIA24485 — Mastcam-Z view of the western delta from the landing area. https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverance-view-of-the-delta-in-jezero-crater/"}]},{"title":"10. Summary of findings","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Jezero Crater is best read as a compound geologic archive. An ancient impact basin later hosted river inflow, standing water, delta and fan deposition, episodes of high-energy flooding, and post-depositional water–rock interaction. Perseverance has shown that the crater floor also contains igneous rocks, including olivine-rich material, so the site records both Mars’ internal magmatic history and its surface hydrologic history. The scientific strength of Jezero lies in connecting orbital mineral maps to rover-scale stratigraphy, geochemistry, subsurface radar, and documented cores while keeping unresolved questions—especially lake duration, carbonate origin, absolute ages, and biosignature interpretation—explicit."}]}],"references":["[1] NASA, 2022. “NASA’s Perseverance Rover Investigates Geologically Rich Mars Terrain.” Mars 2020/Perseverance mission news. Jezero is ~45 km wide; the delta records river–lake deposition; the crater-floor campaign identified igneous rocks. https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasas-perseverance-rover-investigates-geologically-rich-mars-terrain/","[2] Brown, A. J., Viviano, C. E., & Goudge, T. A., 2020. “Olivine-Carbonate Mineralogy of the Jezero Crater Region.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 125, e2019JE006011. DOI: 10.1029/2019JE006011. https://doi.org/10.1029/2019JE006011","[3] Mangold, N., et al., 2021. “Perseverance rover reveals an ancient delta-lake system and flood deposits at Jezero crater, Mars.” Science 374. DOI: 10.1126/science.abl4051. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abl4051","[4] Farley, K. A., et al., 2022. “Aqueously altered igneous rocks sampled on the floor of Jezero crater, Mars.” Science 377. DOI: 10.1126/science.abo2196. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abo2196","[5] Liu, Y., et al., 2022. “An olivine cumulate outcrop on the floor of Jezero crater, Mars.” Science 377. DOI: 10.1126/science.abo2756. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abo2756","[6] Stack, K. M., et al., 2024. “Sedimentology and Stratigraphy of the Shenandoah Formation, Western Fan, Jezero Crater, Mars.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 129, e2023JE008187. DOI: 10.1029/2023JE008187. https://doi.org/10.1029/2023JE008187","[7] NASA Science, n.d. “Perseverance Science Highlights.” Mars 2020/Perseverance mission science summary, including crater-floor, delta, lakebed, and high-energy flood observations. https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-2020-perseverance/science-highlights/","[8] NASA Planetary Data System, Geosciences Node, 2026. “Mars 2020 Perseverance Rover Mission.” Release 16 mission and instrument archives. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/index.htm","[9] NASA Planetary Data System, Geosciences Node, 2026. “MRO: CRISM.” Compact Reconnaissance Imaging Spectrometer for Mars archive and documentation. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/crism.htm","[10] NASA Science, 2021. “Perseverance View of the Delta in Jezero Crater.” Mastcam-Z image record, PIA24485. https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverance-view-of-the-delta-in-jezero-crater/"],"quickLinks":[{"label":"Mars 2020 PDS mission archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/index.htm"},{"label":"PIXL archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/pixl.htm"},{"label":"SHERLOC archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/sherloc.htm"},{"label":"SuperCam archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/supercam.htm"},{"label":"RIMFAX archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mars2020/rimfax.htm"},{"label":"MRO CRISM archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/crism.htm"},{"label":"Perseverance delta image, PIA24485","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverance-view-of-the-delta-in-jezero-crater/"}],"kicker":"FIELD NOTE / 01","query":"Jezero Crater Perseverance delta Mars","localImage":null},"gale":{"title":"Gale Crater","subtitle":"Mount Sharp stratigraphy, ancient lakes, environmental change, and the Curiosity traverse","basis":"Observation basis. This is a remote field note compiled from NASA spacecraft/rover observations, archived mission data, and peer-reviewed planetary-science literature. It does not represent a physical field visit.","meta":[{"label":"SITE","value":"Gale Crater, Aeolis region"},{"label":"APPROX. CENTER","value":"5.4° S, 137.8° E"},{"label":"SCALE","value":"~154 km crater diameter"},{"label":"PRIMARY MISSION","value":"Mars Science Laboratory Curiosity (landed Aug 2012)"}],"sections":[{"title":"1. Site identification and geographical setting","items":[{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Gale is a ~154 km (96 mi) impact crater near the Martian equator, close to the transition between the heavily cratered southern highlands and the lower northern plains. A central mound, Aeolis Mons (Mount Sharp), occupies a large part of the crater interior and rises about 5.5 km above the northern crater floor. [1] [2]"},{"type":"p","text":"Scientific context. The mound is composed of a thick sequence of layered deposits. Because individual strata differ in texture, mineralogy, resistance to erosion, and depositional style, Mount Sharp exposes a vertical environmental record that Curiosity can read by driving progressively upward through younger rocks. [1] [2]"},{"type":"p","text":"Curiosity landed on the northern crater floor, then traversed through plains and ancient sedimentary rocks before reaching the lower slopes of Mount Sharp. The mission therefore samples both material deposited on the crater floor and the layered mound itself rather than a single geologic unit."}]},{"title":"2. Field observations from Curiosity and orbital imagery","items":[{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Orbital images show a crater with a sharply defined rim, a central layered mound, valleys cut into the rim, and fan-like or channel-related deposits near the floor. At rover scale, Curiosity has documented conglomerates, cross-bedded sandstones, laminated mudstones, fracture fills, veins, resistant ridges, and wind-shaped surfaces. [1] [2] [5]"},{"type":"p","text":"Mission record. At Yellowknife Bay, Curiosity drilled fine-grained mudstone in the Sheepbed member. Sedimentary textures and geochemistry were interpreted as products of an ancient fluvial–lacustrine system. The rocks contain detrital basaltic components together with clay minerals, calcium sulfates, iron-bearing phases, and amorphous material. [3] [4]"},{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Farther along the traverse, tilted and cross-bedded sandstones occur beside finely laminated mudstones. The association records streams and deltas delivering sediment into standing water, followed by quieter lake-bottom accumulation. Lower Mount Sharp contains repeated lake-related mudstones of the Murray formation. [5] [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"Scientific interpretation. The preserved facies indicate that Gale repeatedly hosted rivers, deltas, and lakes rather than a single short-lived flooding event. Sedimentary accumulation, burial, cementation, fracturing, groundwater alteration, and later erosion all contributed to the outcrops now exposed to the rover. [5] [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"Surface-process note. Modern wind continues to modify the site. Sand ripples, dunes, dust coatings, ventifacts, and differential erosion can obscure or accentuate primary sedimentary structures. Curiosity observations therefore combine fresh drill material and abraded or brushed surfaces with broader landscape imaging."}]},{"title":"3. Geological interpretation and stratigraphic history","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"A large impact excavated Gale more than three billion years ago and created the topographic basin later occupied by sedimentary deposits. [1] [2]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Runoff from the crater rim transported sediment onto the floor. Rivers and deltas fed lakes, depositing conglomerate, sandstone, silt, and mud under different flow regimes. [3] [5]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Lake-bottom mud accumulated repeatedly. At least part of lower Mount Sharp is built from sediment deposited in long-lived or recurring lacustrine systems rather than exclusively wind-blown material. [5] [6]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Burial and diagenesis altered the sediment after deposition. Mineral-filled veins, nodules, cemented zones, and changes in oxidation state show that groundwater continued to move through the rocks after primary sedimentation. [4] [6] [7]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Later erosion removed large volumes of crater fill and exhumed Mount Sharp. Wind is a major agent in the present landscape and helps expose the layered record. [5]"},{"type":"p","text":"Interpretive caution. Mount Sharp is not a simple continuous timeline in which every meter equals a fixed interval of time. Unconformities, erosional surfaces, lateral facies changes, and diagenetic overprints can remove, compress, or modify parts of the record. Stratigraphic position gives relative order, but absolute ages remain less precise."}]},{"title":"4. Mineralogy and composition","items":[{"type":"p","text":"CheMin mineralogy. The CheMin X-ray diffraction instrument directly identifies crystalline minerals in drilled powders. Yellowknife Bay mudstones contain basalt-derived minerals together with smectite clay, calcium sulfate, iron sulfide/oxide phases, and substantial amorphous material. Clay formation indicates chemical alteration in the presence of water. [4]"},{"type":"p","text":"Lower Mount Sharp. CheMin analyses of Murray formation mudstones show a mineralogically variable lacustrine succession. Clay minerals, feldspars, pyroxenes, iron oxides, sulfates, and amorphous components change with stratigraphy, providing evidence for shifts in source material, water chemistry, and post-depositional alteration. [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"Orbital-to-rover connection. Before landing, orbital spectroscopy highlighted lower-Mount-Sharp units enriched in clay minerals, hematite, and sulfates. Curiosity has tested these orbital predictions at the surface, including clay-bearing terrain and hematite-rich ridge materials, and has encountered increasingly sulfate-rich strata higher on the mound. [10]"},{"type":"p","text":"Organic chemistry. SAM heating experiments on ancient mudstones detected organic molecules released from the samples, including sulfur-bearing and aromatic/aliphatic products. The result establishes preservation of indigenous or ancient organic carbon-bearing material in the rocks, but the measurements do not establish a biological origin. [8]"}]},{"title":"5. Water history and ancient environmental conditions","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Established evidence. Rounded clasts in conglomerates, delta-like sedimentary geometry, lake mudstones, clay minerals, sulfate veins, and chemical alteration collectively demonstrate repeated water activity in Gale. The record spans flowing surface water, standing lakes, and later groundwater circulation. [3] [4] [5]"},{"type":"p","text":"Habitability at Yellowknife Bay. The Sheepbed mudstone was interpreted as having formed in a low-salinity, near-neutral-water environment with key elements needed by known microorganisms and possible chemical energy sources. This is one of Curiosity’s strongest results: an ancient Martian environment could satisfy multiple habitability requirements even though no direct evidence of life was found. [3]"},{"type":"p","text":"Lake chemistry. Redox-sensitive measurements across lake deposits indicate that the ancient lake could have contained chemically different water zones, analogous in concept to stratified terrestrial lakes. Such gradients matter because they create distinct energy and preservation environments within the same basin. [7]"},{"type":"p","text":"Long-term change. Higher stratigraphic levels document a broad transition toward more sulfate-rich rocks. Sulfates can form or be concentrated under evaporative, oxidizing, or groundwater-related conditions, so the mineral transition is used to investigate how Mars shifted from comparatively wet environments toward drier surface conditions. The transition was not necessarily monotonic; local wet episodes and groundwater alteration continued. [6] [10]"}]},{"title":"6. NASA mission observations and instruments","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"Mastcam and navigation cameras: landscape mosaics, bedding geometry, grain-scale context, color, clast shape, and stratigraphic relationships. [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MAHLI: hand-lens-scale imaging of rock textures, veins, drill fines, and sedimentary fabrics. [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"APXS: bulk elemental chemistry on rocks, soils, brushed surfaces, and drill tailings. [9] [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"ChemCam: laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy plus remote micro-imaging for stand-off composition and texture. [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"CheMin: X-ray diffraction and fluorescence for definitive mineral identification in drilled samples. [4] [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"SAM: evolved-gas, mass-spectrometric, chromatographic, and tunable-laser measurements of volatiles, organics, isotopes, and atmospheric gases. [8] [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"DAN, REMS, RAD and rover engineering observations: hydrogen-bearing material in the shallow subsurface, weather, radiation environment, and traverse context. [10]"}]},{"title":"7. Scientific significance","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Gale Crater is scientifically important because Curiosity has transformed a remote orbital prediction—layered rocks that might record wet conditions—into a ground-truthed stratigraphic sequence. The rover has shown that habitable fluvial and lake environments existed, that clay-bearing mudstones formed and were preserved, that groundwater later modified the rocks, and that the mound records substantial environmental change. [3] [4] [5] [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"The site also demonstrates why Mars habitability cannot be reduced to the question “Was there water?” Curiosity measures mineralogy, elemental chemistry, redox state, organics, sedimentary textures, radiation, and present weather. These datasets allow habitability to be treated as an environmental system with water chemistry, energy sources, preservation conditions, and duration. [3] [7] [8]"}]},{"title":"8. Uncertainties and data limitations","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"The total duration and continuity of Gale’s lakes remain model dependent. Sedimentary evidence supports repeated or long-lived aqueous episodes, but rock exposure does not preserve every interval of time. [5]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"The precise origin of Mount Sharp remains a synthesis of deposition and erosion. Sediments may include lacustrine, fluvial, aeolian, and other contributions that vary laterally and vertically. [5] [6]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Diagenesis can overprint primary mineralogy. Veins, cements, oxidation, and groundwater reactions may record conditions long after original sediment deposition. [6] [7]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"SAM organic detections do not specify whether the precursor carbon was biological, meteoritic, hydrothermal, atmospheric, or another abiotic source. [8]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Curiosity analyzes a traverse only meters to kilometers wide through a crater 154 km across. Orbital data provide regional coverage but at lower spatial and compositional specificity."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Absolute ages are mostly inferred from regional chronology and crater statistics. Curiosity has not produced a complete radiometric timeline for every stratigraphic unit."}]},{"title":"9. Relevant NASA data and original resources","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"MSL/Curiosity PDS archive — Mission instrument archives, release notes, and links to the MSL Analyst’s Notebook. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/msl/index.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"APXS archive — Raw and derived elemental chemistry products, including oxide-abundance tables. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/msl/apxs.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Curiosity science instruments — NASA descriptions of the rover’s imaging, chemistry, mineralogy, atmospheric, radiation, and subsurface instruments. https://science.nasa.gov/mission/msl-curiosity/science-instruments/"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Gale Crater orbital context — NASA composite orbital view showing the crater and Mount Sharp. https://science.nasa.gov/resource/destination-gale-crater-in-august-2012/"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MRO CRISM archive — Orbital mineral mapping used to define clay-, hematite-, and sulfate-bearing targets. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/crism.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MGS MOLA topography — Global and regional elevation products for crater and mound geometry. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/mola.html"}]},{"title":"10. Summary of findings","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Gale Crater preserves one of the most detailed ground-truthed environmental records yet examined on Mars. Curiosity has documented ancient streams, deltas, lakes, lake-bottom mudstones, clay formation, redox gradients, groundwater alteration, organic carbon-bearing materials, and a stratigraphic shift toward more sulfate-rich rocks. Mount Sharp functions as a layered archive rather than a single landform of one origin. Its scientific value comes from following environmental change through stratigraphy while distinguishing primary depositional conditions from later diagenesis and modern wind erosion."}]}],"references":["[1] NASA, 2012. “Destination Gale Crater in August 2012.” NASA/JPL-Caltech mission image and site description. Gale is ~154 km across and Mount Sharp rises ~5.5 km above the crater floor. https://science.nasa.gov/resource/destination-gale-crater-in-august-2012/","[2] NASA Science, 2012. “Gale Crater’s History Book.” Orbital context for the crater and central layered mound. https://science.nasa.gov/resource/gale-craters-history-book/","[3] Grotzinger, J. P., et al., 2014. “A habitable fluvio-lacustrine environment at Yellowknife Bay, Gale crater, Mars.” Science 343. DOI: 10.1126/science.1242777. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1242777","[4] Vaniman, D. T., et al., 2014. “Mineralogy of a mudstone at Yellowknife Bay, Gale crater, Mars.” Science 343. DOI: 10.1126/science.1243480. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1243480","[5] Grotzinger, J. P., et al., 2015. “Deposition, exhumation, and paleoclimate of an ancient lake deposit, Gale crater, Mars.” Science 350. DOI: 10.1126/science.aac7575. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aac7575","[6] Rampe, E. B., et al., 2017. “Mineralogy of an ancient lacustrine mudstone succession from the Murray formation, Gale crater, Mars.” Earth and Planetary Science Letters 471, 172–185. DOI: 10.1016/j.epsl.2017.04.021. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.epsl.2017.04.021","[7] Hurowitz, J. A., et al., 2017. “Redox stratification of an ancient lake in Gale crater, Mars.” Science 356, eaah6849. DOI: 10.1126/science.aah6849. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aah6849","[8] Eigenbrode, J. L., et al., 2018. “Organic matter preserved in 3-billion-year-old mudstones at Gale crater, Mars.” Science 360. DOI: 10.1126/science.aas9185. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aas9185","[9] NASA Planetary Data System, Geosciences Node, 2026. “Mars Science Laboratory (MSL) / Curiosity mission archives.” Instrument data and Analyst’s Notebook access. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/msl/index.htm","[10] NASA Science, n.d. “Curiosity Science Instruments.” Instrument descriptions for Mastcam, MAHLI, APXS, ChemCam, CheMin, SAM, DAN, RAD, REMS and navigation systems. https://science.nasa.gov/mission/msl-curiosity/science-instruments/"],"quickLinks":[{"label":"MSL/Curiosity PDS archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/msl/index.htm"},{"label":"APXS archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/msl/apxs.htm"},{"label":"Curiosity science instruments","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/msl-curiosity/science-instruments/"},{"label":"Gale Crater orbital context","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/destination-gale-crater-in-august-2012/"},{"label":"MRO CRISM archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/crism.htm"},{"label":"MGS MOLA topography","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/mola.html"}],"kicker":"FIELD NOTE / 02","query":"Gale Crater Mount Sharp Curiosity Mars","localImage":null},"olympus":{"title":"Olympus Mons","subtitle":"Shield-volcano architecture, lava-flow history, summit collapse, and Tharsis tectono-volcanic context","basis":"Observation basis. This is a remote field note compiled from NASA spacecraft/rover observations, archived mission data, and peer-reviewed planetary-science literature. It does not represent a physical field visit.","meta":[{"label":"SITE","value":"Olympus Mons, northwestern Tharsis"},{"label":"OFFICIAL CENTER","value":"18.4° N, 226.0° E (+east)"},{"label":"SCALE","value":"~600 km volcanic base; official feature extent ~648 km"},{"label":"OBSERVATION MODE","value":"Orbital remote sensing; no landed mission"}],"sections":[{"title":"1. Site identification and geographical setting","items":[{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Olympus Mons is the largest known volcano in the Solar System and a classic low-slope shield volcano on the northwestern margin of the Tharsis volcanic province. NASA descriptions place the volcanic base at roughly 600 km across and the summit about 27 km above the reference/adjacent plains. A steep basal escarpment, locally several kilometers high, sharply separates much of the edifice from surrounding terrain. [1] [2]"},{"type":"p","text":"Cartographic note. The U.S. Geological Survey planetary gazetteer gives the named feature a center near 18.4° N, 226.0° E and a conventional mapped diameter of about 648 km. Different reported “diameters” are not necessarily contradictory: some measure the named feature boundary, others the main shield or basal scarp. [3]"},{"type":"p","text":"Regional setting. Olympus Mons stands west of the main Tharsis Montes chain. Its immense load sits on a flexed lithosphere and is surrounded by plains, a flexural basin in places, and the distinctive ridged and grooved Olympus aureole. This context is central to interpreting both volcanic construction and large-scale gravitational deformation. [1] [6] [7]"}]},{"title":"2. Field observations from orbital imagery and topography","items":[{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Viking, Mars Odyssey, Mars Global Surveyor, Mars Express, and Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter images show thousands of overlapping lava-flow surfaces on the flanks. Individual flows include leveed channels, sinuous channels, sheet-like lobes, pressure-related ridges, and flow fronts. In places younger flows overlie or cross the basal scarp and older surfaces. [1] [5]"},{"type":"p","text":"Topographic observation. MOLA elevation data reveal an extremely broad edifice with gentle regional slopes compared with its total height. The contrast—huge relief spread across hundreds of kilometers—is characteristic of shield volcanism produced by repeated effusion of relatively mobile lava rather than by construction of a steep composite cone. [1] [9]"},{"type":"p","text":"Summit observation. The summit is not a single bowl. Detailed mapping resolves a nested caldera complex with six overlapping collapse pits occupying roughly 65 by 80 km. The floors contain wrinkle ridges, graben, lava surfaces, and evidence for more than a kilometer of post-formation elevation change in parts of the caldera system. [4]"},{"type":"p","text":"Basal and aureole observation. The shield is bordered by a prominent scarp and by broad aureole deposits with ridged, grooved, and blocky textures. Some aureole units extend far beyond the modern shield. Their morphology records large-scale flank failure or gravity-driven deformation, but the detailed mechanism and role of weak layers or volatiles remain debated. [1] [7]"}]},{"title":"3. Geological interpretation and volcanic history","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"Repeated low-viscosity lava effusion built the shield incrementally. The volcano is a stack of many flow fields rather than the product of one eruption. [1] [5]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Flow morphology changes through the preserved sequence. Mapping finds channel-fed flows to be common and interprets younger flow fields as reflecting different effusion behavior from some older, more tube-dominated surfaces. [5]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Summit calderas formed through multiple episodes of magma withdrawal and roof collapse, not one catastrophic summit event. Overlapping pits and lava-lake surfaces record repeated reservoir reorganization. [1] [4]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"The weight of the growing volcano flexed the crust/lithosphere. Misoriented older lava flows in the surrounding basin indicate that regional slopes changed after those flows formed, consistent with later subsidence centered on Olympus Mons. [6]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Large aureole deposits and the basal scarp indicate major flank deformation or collapse. The mapped geometry is consistent with gravitational spreading/sliding, although the exact mechanical conditions are unresolved. [1] [7]"},{"type":"p","text":"Age interpretation. Most age estimates for Olympus Mons are derived from crater size-frequency statistics and stratigraphic relations, not laboratory radiometric dating. The edifice includes ancient constructional history as well as very young surfaces by Martian geologic standards. Some mapped late-stage volcanic and tectonic surfaces are estimated at hundreds to tens of millions of years old, but individual values depend on the chronology model and surface definition. [6] [8]"}]},{"title":"4. Lava-flow morphology, rock composition, and mineralogical constraints","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Morphologic evidence. Long, overlapping flow lobes and channels demonstrate dominantly effusive volcanism. In a shield volcano, repeated emplacement of relatively fluid lava allows the edifice to spread laterally over enormous distances while maintaining low average slopes. [1] [5]"},{"type":"p","text":"Compositional constraint. No lander or rover has analyzed Olympus Mons bedrock in situ. Consequently, its detailed mineralogy is less directly constrained than at Jezero or Gale. Orbital thermal and spectral datasets are interpreted within the broader basaltic volcanic character of Mars, but dust coatings, surface alteration, mixed pixels, and atmospheric effects limit mineral identification at many locations."},{"type":"p","text":"Data implication. For a scientific website, the most defensible “composition” layer for Olympus should distinguish direct measurements—thermal infrared radiance/emissivity, albedo, morphology, topography—from geological inference such as basaltic lava type or volatile content. Morphology is exceptionally well constrained; mineral proportions are not."},{"type":"p","text":"Thermal remote sensing. Mars Odyssey THEMIS provides daytime/nighttime thermal-infrared imaging and visible context. Thermal behavior helps separate dust, rockier surfaces, and thermophysical units, while MOLA provides the elevation framework needed to interpret slope-controlled lava emplacement. [9] [10]"}]},{"title":"5. Water history and environmental conditions","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Established evidence. The construction of Olympus Mons does not require lakes, rivers, or other surface-water environments; its primary record is volcanic. There is no landed geochemical record demonstrating long-lived surface water on the shield."},{"type":"p","text":"Debated observations. Some channels and streamlined forms on or near the eastern flank have been interpreted as evidence that water-related erosion occurred after emplacement of some volcanic surfaces. Other channels can be volcanic in origin, and separating lava-cut, collapse-related, glacial, and fluvial morphologies from orbit is not always straightforward. [8]"},{"type":"p","text":"Aureole and volatile hypotheses. Models for the giant flank deposits sometimes invoke weak substrate layers, ice, or water-rich sediments to facilitate gravitational spreading. These are mechanical hypotheses, not direct observations of standing water. The NASA description of the aureole appropriately treats its origin as controversial. [1]"},{"type":"p","text":"Environmental significance. Olympus Mons therefore contributes to Mars water history mainly indirectly: it records how volcanism, crustal loading, possible volatile-bearing substrates, and climate-sensitive erosion may have interacted. It should not be presented as an ancient lake or habitable-sedimentary site analogous to Jezero or Gale."}]},{"title":"6. NASA and partner-mission observations","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"Viking Orbiter: broad regional mosaics established the shield, basal scarp, summit complex, and aureole morphology at planetary scale. [1]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Global Surveyor MOLA: laser altimetry quantifies relief, slope, caldera depth/elevation, and flexural topography; MEGDR products provide gridded elevation maps. [9]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Odyssey THEMIS: thermal-infrared and visible imaging maps lava surfaces and thermophysical contrasts; classic THEMIS images show stacked flow lobes on the flanks. [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Express HRSC: stereo color imagery provides high-resolution morphology and topography used in detailed lava-flow mapping. [5]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter CTX/HiRISE: high-resolution images reveal flow textures, faults, small craters, mass-wasting features, and fine-scale caldera structures."},{"type":"bullet","text":"USGS planetary mapping: standardized geologic mapping integrates image, topographic, and stratigraphic evidence into reproducible map units and contacts. [4] [7]"}]},{"title":"7. Scientific significance","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Olympus Mons is a natural laboratory for the mechanics of giant shield volcanoes under Martian gravity, lithospheric conditions, and long-lived hotspot-like volcanism. Its flow fields record changes in effusion style, its nested calderas record repeated magma-reservoir collapse, and its enormous load provides a test of lithospheric flexure and subsidence. [4] [5] [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"The volcano also constrains the longevity of Martian internal activity. Crater-count studies and stratigraphic mapping indicate that volcanism and deformation persisted well into the Amazonian period, implying that Mars remained volcanically active long after the earliest highland crust formed. Because those ages are model ages rather than returned-sample radiometric ages, they are scientifically useful but should be displayed with uncertainty. [6] [8]"}]},{"title":"8. Uncertainties and data limitations","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"No surface mission has sampled Olympus Mons. Rock chemistry, grain-scale textures, isotopic composition, and absolute radiometric ages remain unknown in situ."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Reported height and diameter values depend on the reference datum and on whether the measurement follows the named feature, basal scarp, volcanic apron, or surrounding flexural basin. [1] [2] [3]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Crater-count ages are model dependent and can be biased by resurfacing, secondary impacts, incomplete crater preservation, and uncertainty in Mars’ impact-flux chronology. [6] [8]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"The origin of the aureole is not uniquely resolved. Gravity spreading, landsliding, substrate weakness, and possible volatile-related processes have all been considered. [1] [7]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Some channel-like landforms may be produced by lava, water, ice-related processes, collapse, or combinations; morphology alone can be non-unique. [8]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Dust and alteration coatings reduce the ability of orbital spectroscopy to determine the primary mineralogy of lava flows at all locations."}]},{"title":"9. Relevant NASA data and original resources","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"MOLA topography archive — Mars Global Surveyor laser-altimetry profiles and maintained gridded topography products. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/mola.html"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MOLA MEGDR / PDS4 topography — Global topographic maps at multiple resolutions; legacy dataset MGS-M-MOLA-5-MEGDR-L3-V1.0, DOI 10.17189/1519460. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/megdr.html"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Odyssey / THEMIS archive — Thermal-emission imaging and mission data used for lava-flow and thermophysical mapping. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/odyssey/"},{"type":"bullet","text":"NASA Viking Olympus Mons mosaic — Public-domain regional morphology image and description. https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/olympus-mons/"},{"type":"bullet","text":"USGS Olympus Mons caldera geologic map — Scientific Investigations Map 3470 with downloadable map sheet and GIS products. https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/sim3470"},{"type":"bullet","text":"USGS planetary gazetteer — Official nomenclature, location, and feature extent. https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/4453"}]},{"title":"10. Summary of findings","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Olympus Mons is an immense, low-slope shield built by repeated effusive eruptions, later modified by summit collapse, flank deformation, lithospheric flexure, impacts, and erosion. Its lava-flow morphology and topography are well constrained from orbital data, while its detailed rock mineralogy and absolute chronology remain comparatively uncertain because no landed mission has sampled it. The most scientifically responsible interpretation emphasizes repeated flow emplacement, a complex nested caldera, large-scale gravitational and tectonic deformation, and Amazonian-age activity while treating water-related channels, aureole mechanics, and exact eruption ages as active or model-dependent questions."}]}],"references":["[1] NASA Science / NASA JPL / USGS, 1998. “Olympus Mons.” Viking Orbiter mosaic and morphology description: ~27 km high, >600 km across at the base, basal scarp up to ~6 km, lava flows, aureole, and summit collapse features. https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/olympus-mons/","[2] NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, 2024. “NASA’s Mars Odyssey Captures Huge Volcano, Nears 100,000 Orbits.” Odyssey horizon view; Olympus Mons base ~600 km and relief ~27 km. https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-mars-odyssey-captures-huge-volcano-nears-100000-orbits/","[3] U.S. Geological Survey, Gazetteer of Planetary Nomenclature, current entry. “Olympus Mons.” Official planetary feature location and extent; center near 18.4° N, 226.0° E in the +east convention. https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/4453","[4] Mouginis-Mark, P. J., 2021. “Geologic map of Olympus Mons caldera, Mars.” U.S. Geological Survey Scientific Investigations Map 3470, scale 1:200,000. DOI: 10.3133/sim3470. https://doi.org/10.3133/sim3470","[5] Bleacher, J. E., et al., 2007. “Olympus Mons, Mars: Inferred changes in late Amazonian aged effusive activity from lava flow mapping of Mars Express High Resolution Stereo Camera data.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 112. DOI: 10.1029/2006JE002826. https://doi.org/10.1029/2006JE002826","[6] Chadwick, J., McGovern, P., Simpson, M., & Reeves, A., 2015. “Late Amazonian subsidence and magmatism of Olympus Mons, Mars.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 120, 1585–1595. DOI: 10.1002/2015JE004875. https://doi.org/10.1002/2015JE004875","[7] Morris, E. C., & Tanaka, K. L., 1994. “Geologic maps of the Olympus Mons region of Mars.” U.S. Geological Survey Miscellaneous Investigations Series Map I-2327. DOI: 10.3133/i2327. https://doi.org/10.3133/i2327","[8] Basilevsky, A. T., et al., 2006. “Geologically recent tectonic, volcanic and fluvial activity on the eastern flank of the Olympus Mons volcano, Mars.” Geophysical Research Letters 33. DOI: 10.1029/2006GL026396. https://doi.org/10.1029/2006GL026396","[9] NASA Planetary Data System, Geosciences Node, 2026. “Mars Global Surveyor: MOLA.” Altimetry and global topographic map archive. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/mola.html","[10] NASA Planetary Data System, Geosciences Node, 2026. “2001 Mars Odyssey.” Mission data entry point including THEMIS thermal-infrared and visible imaging. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/odyssey/"],"quickLinks":[{"label":"MOLA topography archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/mola.html"},{"label":"MOLA MEGDR / PDS4 topography","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/megdr.html"},{"label":"Mars Odyssey / THEMIS archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/odyssey/"},{"label":"NASA Viking Olympus Mons mosaic","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/olympus-mons/"},{"label":"USGS Olympus Mons caldera geologic map","url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/sim3470"},{"label":"USGS planetary gazetteer","url":"https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/4453"}],"kicker":"FIELD NOTE / 03","query":"Olympus Mons Mars NASA","localImage":"assets/olympus-mons.jpg"},"valles":{"title":"Valles Marineris","subtitle":"Canyon morphology, crustal deformation, exposed stratigraphy, sulfate-bearing deposits, and formation models","basis":"Observation basis. This is a remote field note compiled from NASA spacecraft/rover observations, archived mission data, and peer-reviewed planetary-science literature. It does not represent a physical field visit.","meta":[{"label":"SITE","value":"Valles Marineris canyon system"},{"label":"OFFICIAL CENTER","value":"14.01° S, 301.41° E (+east)"},{"label":"SCALE","value":"~3,761 km official feature length; up to ~600 km wide"},{"label":"OBSERVATION MODE","value":"Orbital imaging, spectroscopy, radar/topography; no landed mission"}],"sections":[{"title":"1. Site identification and geographical setting","items":[{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Valles Marineris is a linked system of troughs and chasmata extending across equatorial Mars east of the Tharsis volcanic rise. NASA descriptions give a total span of roughly 3,870 km, widths reaching about 600 km, and maximum relief approaching 9 km. The USGS named-feature entry gives a standardized length of 3,761.28 km and a center near 14.01° S, 301.41° E. [1] [2]"},{"type":"p","text":"Geographical setting. The western system begins near Noctis Labyrinthus, a complex of intersecting fault-bounded depressions near Tharsis. Eastward, major troughs including Ius, Tithonium, Melas, Candor, Ophir, Coprates, Capri, and Eos Chasmata broaden, merge, and connect toward chaotic terrain and outflow-channel provinces. [3]"},{"type":"p","text":"Scale note. Valles Marineris is not a single river-cut canyon analogous to one terrestrial valley. It is a tectono-erosional canyon system made of multiple structural troughs, interior basins, wall slopes, layered mounds, landslide deposits, and channels that record several processes operating over a long interval. [3] [4]"}]},{"title":"2. Field observations from orbital imagery and topography","items":[{"type":"p","text":"NASA observation. Viking, CTX, HiRISE, and other orbital images show steep walls exposing kilometer-scale crustal sections, fault-bounded trough margins, terraces, spur-and-gully topography, collapsed blocks, talus, broad landslide aprons, and locally chaotic floor materials. Large interior mounds of layered deposits rise from the floors of several chasmata. [1] [3]"},{"type":"p","text":"Topographic observation. MOLA data resolve extreme relief between plateau surfaces and canyon floors and show that different chasmata have distinct floor elevations, widths, wall geometries, and connections. The canyon system therefore cannot be explained by uniform vertical incision alone. [4] [11]"},{"type":"p","text":"Layered-deposit observation. Central Valles Marineris contains thick, light-toned, finely layered deposits. HiRISE images reveal bedding at meter-to-submeter scales, erosional benches, cliffs, dark mantles, dunes, and local unconformities. CRISM spectra demonstrate that many of these layers contain hydrated sulfate minerals. [7] [9] [10]"},{"type":"p","text":"Mass-wasting observation. Wall slopes contain slumps, rockfalls, debris aprons, and enormous landslide deposits. These gravity-driven materials are younger than the canyon walls they detach from and can bury older floor deposits, complicating reconstruction of the original trough geometry. [3]"}]},{"title":"3. Geological interpretation and formation history","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Established structural evidence. The geometry of faults, graben-like depressions, and trough margins shows that crustal extension and subsidence are fundamental to Valles Marineris. The system is spatially associated with the Tharsis rise, whose volcanic and tectonic loading strongly affected stresses in the surrounding lithosphere. [3] [4]"},{"type":"p","text":"Model interpretation. Mechanical models indicate that simple horizontal stretching alone does not reproduce all observed features. Andrews-Hanna’s models favor a combination of extension with substantial vertical accommodation or subsidence, with deep-seated deformation contributing to trough formation while limiting the amount of surface extension required. [4]"},{"type":"p","text":"Regional stress hypothesis. One proposed mechanism is that stresses from the Tharsis load were focused near the buried crustal dichotomy boundary, helping localize fracturing where Valles Marineris developed. A related model invokes a super-isostatic Tharsis plateau, intrusion, sediment loading, and subsidence to explain the great depth of the troughs. [5] [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"Erosional modification. After structural troughs formed, wall collapse, landsliding, aeolian erosion, channel activity, groundwater processes, and sediment accumulation enlarged and modified them. Thus, tectonics created much of the accommodation space, but erosion and sedimentation produced many of the detailed forms visible today. [3] [6]"}]},{"title":"4. Exposed geological layers, mineralogy, and composition","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Crustal exposures. Canyon walls expose long vertical sections through ancient Martian crust. Darker wall rocks are commonly interpreted as basaltic volcanic or intrusive materials and their altered equivalents, but exact composition varies and is difficult to determine continuously along thousands of kilometers of wall exposure."},{"type":"p","text":"CRISM mineralogy. Interior layered deposits in western Candor Chasma contain widespread monohydrated sulfates, nanophase ferric oxide, basaltic/pyroxene-bearing sand layers, and localized polyhydrated sulfates higher in the section. Ferric minerals including hematite occur within sulfate-bearing units. [7]"},{"type":"p","text":"Hydration state. In Melas Chasma and elsewhere, CRISM distinguishes monohydrated sulfate layers from more strongly hydrated sulfate layers because they have different infrared absorption features. Changes in hydration state may reflect changes in water chemistry at deposition, later hydration/dehydration, burial, heating, or pressure effects. [9] [10]"},{"type":"p","text":"Spatial diversity. Some thin wall-rock deposits contain clays or mixtures of hydrated minerals rather than the sulfate-dominated mineralogy of the largest interior mounds. This suggests that more than one aqueous process, chemical environment, or alteration episode operated in the canyon system. [9]"}]},{"title":"5. Water history and environmental conditions","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Established evidence. Hydrated sulfates are direct mineralogical evidence that water participated in the formation or alteration of major sedimentary units. Their abundance and stratigraphic organization show that aqueous chemistry affected parts of Valles Marineris after or during canyon development. [7] [9]"},{"type":"p","text":"Hydrologic interpretation. One influential model for Candor Chasma proposes that groundwater discharged into the troughs, evaporated under saline conditions, precipitated sulfate minerals, and trapped wind-blown dust and sand to build and cement layered deposits. The model reproduces aspects of deposit thickness and distribution but is not unique. [7]"},{"type":"p","text":"Alternative depositional possibilities. Other layered units may include airfall dust, volcanic ash, aeolian sediment, mass-wasting debris, lake or playa deposits, and material altered after deposition. Nearly horizontal bedding alone does not prove a deep standing lake; mineralogy, geometry, facies relationships, and topography must be evaluated together. [7] [9]"},{"type":"p","text":"Fluvial evidence. Some streamlined forms, channels, and eastern connections to chaotic terrain and outflow systems indicate episodes when flowing water or water release modified the canyon environment. However, the canyon system as a whole is not interpreted as having been excavated primarily by one giant river. [1] [3]"}]},{"title":"6. NASA orbital investigations and datasets","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"Viking Orbiters: regional mosaics that established the full scale and principal morphology of the canyon system. [1]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Global Surveyor MOLA: elevation profiles and global gridded topography used to quantify wall relief, trough depth, plateau elevation, and regional gradients. [11]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Odyssey THEMIS: thermal-infrared and visible mapping of floor materials, layered deposits, dunes, and thermophysical units."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter CTX: broad high-resolution context for structural mapping, landslides, channels, and stratigraphic relationships."},{"type":"bullet","text":"MRO HiRISE: sub-meter imaging of bedding, scarps, boulders, dunes, faults, erosional textures, and candidate aqueous deposits. [9]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MRO CRISM: hyperspectral mapping of sulfates, clays, ferric minerals, pyroxene, and other phases that connect morphology to aqueous and volcanic processes. [7] [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"SHARAD and stereo topography, where coverage permits: subsurface or structural context and high-resolution digital terrain models that test layer attitudes and canyon-wall geometry."}]},{"title":"7. Scientific significance","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Valles Marineris is one of the best natural cross sections through the Martian crust. Its walls expose materials otherwise buried beneath the plateau, while its floors preserve younger sedimentary, volcanic, mass-wasting, and aqueous deposits. The system therefore links deep crustal structure to surface environmental history. [3]"},{"type":"p","text":"It is also a major test case for planetary tectonics. Models must explain why troughs are so deep, why surface extension is limited relative to total subsidence, how Tharsis stresses were localized, and how later collapse and sedimentation modified the system. No single process accounts for all observations. [4] [5] [6]"},{"type":"p","text":"The sulfate-rich layered deposits are important for paleoclimate and astrobiology because salts can record brine chemistry, evaporation, groundwater discharge, and diagenesis. They do not by themselves prove a persistent habitable lake, but they document water–rock interaction under chemical conditions unlike those of neutral-water lake mudstones at Gale. [7] [9]"}]},{"title":"8. Uncertainties and data limitations","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"The relative contributions of rifting, deep subsidence, collapse, magmatic intrusion, and erosion to initial trough formation remain debated. Mechanical models are constrained by observations but are not direct measurements of the ancient stress field. [4] [5] [6]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Interior layered deposits have multiple plausible origins. Groundwater-evaporite models fit important observations, but some units may include lacustrine, aeolian, airfall, volcanic, or mass-wasting components. [7]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Hydrated mineral detections identify present spectral phases at the surface; they do not always preserve the original hydration state or depositional water chemistry. [9] [10]"},{"type":"bullet","text":"No rover has examined Valles Marineris in situ. Grain-scale sedimentology, precise geochemistry, absolute ages, and subsurface relationships must be inferred from orbital data."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Dust, dark sand mantles, steep slopes, shadows, and mass-wasting cover obscure large portions of the walls and floor and can limit spectral retrievals."},{"type":"bullet","text":"The canyon system spans thousands of kilometers. Processes inferred in one chasma should not automatically be generalized to all chasmata without local evidence."}]},{"title":"9. Relevant NASA data and original resources","items":[{"type":"bullet","text":"MRO CRISM archive — Visible/near-infrared hyperspectral data for sulfate, clay, ferric-mineral, and mafic-mineral mapping. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/crism.htm"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MRO mission / PDS entry point — Archives and documentation for Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter datasets used in canyon mapping. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MOLA topography archive — Laser-altimetry profiles and gridded topographic products for regional relief and canyon geometry. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/mola.html"},{"type":"bullet","text":"MOLA MEGDR topography — Maintained global gridded elevation products; legacy dataset MGS-M-MOLA-5-MEGDR-L3-V1.0, DOI 10.17189/1519460. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/megdr.html"},{"type":"bullet","text":"NASA HiRISE/CRISM sulfate example — Melas Chasma — Layered mono- and polyhydrated sulfate-bearing deposits, PIA21935. https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mixtures-of-sulfates-in-melas-chasma/"},{"type":"bullet","text":"NASA HiRISE layered sediments — Central Valles Marineris hydrated-sulfate-rich layered deposits, PIA25308. https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/layered-sediments-in-valles-marineris-2/"},{"type":"bullet","text":"USGS geologic map — Regional geologic map I-2010 of Valles Marineris. https://doi.org/10.3133/i2010"}]},{"title":"10. Summary of findings","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Valles Marineris is a tectonically initiated and heavily modified canyon system rather than a simple river valley. Extension, deep subsidence, Tharsis-related stresses, faulting, collapse, mass wasting, erosion, and sediment accumulation all contributed to its present form. The walls expose ancient crust, while central chasmata preserve thick layered deposits containing hydrated sulfates and other minerals that record episodes of water–rock interaction and changing brine chemistry. Its greatest scientific value lies in connecting Martian crustal mechanics with a younger sedimentary and aqueous record, while major questions about the exact formation mechanism and the origin of individual layered deposits remain open."}]}],"references":["[1] NASA, 2008. “Valles Marineris: The Grand Canyon of Mars.” NASA image article describing the canyon system at roughly 3,870 km long, up to ~600 km wide, and up to ~9.3 km deep. https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/valles-marineris-grand-canyon-of-mars/","[2] U.S. Geological Survey, Gazetteer of Planetary Nomenclature, current entry. “Valles Marineris.” Official feature length 3761.28 km; center near 14.01° S, 301.41° E. https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/SearchResults?Target=20_Mars","[3] Witbeck, N. E., Tanaka, K. L., & Scott, D. H., 1991. “Geologic map of the Valles Marineris region, Mars.” U.S. Geological Survey Miscellaneous Investigations Series Map I-2010. DOI: 10.3133/i2010. https://doi.org/10.3133/i2010","[4] Andrews-Hanna, J. C., 2012. “The formation of Valles Marineris: 1. Tectonic architecture and the relative roles of extension and subsidence.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 117. DOI: 10.1029/2011JE003953. https://doi.org/10.1029/2011JE003953","[5] Andrews-Hanna, J. C., 2012. “The formation of Valles Marineris: 2. Stress focusing along the buried dichotomy boundary.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 117. DOI: 10.1029/2011JE003954. https://doi.org/10.1029/2011JE003954","[6] Andrews-Hanna, J. C., 2012. “The formation of Valles Marineris: 3. Trough formation through super-isostasy, stress, sedimentation, and subsidence.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 117. DOI: 10.1029/2012JE004059. https://doi.org/10.1029/2012JE004059","[7] Murchie, S. L., et al., 2009. “Evidence for the origin of layered deposits in Candor Chasma, Mars, from mineral composition and hydrologic modeling.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 114. DOI: 10.1029/2009JE003343. https://doi.org/10.1029/2009JE003343","[8] Flahaut, J., et al., 2010. “Identification, distribution and possible origins of sulfates in Capri Chasma (Mars), inferred from CRISM data.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets 115. DOI: 10.1029/2009JE003566. https://doi.org/10.1029/2009JE003566","[9] NASA Science, 2017. “Mixtures of Sulfates in Melas Chasma.” MRO HiRISE/CRISM observation of mono- and polyhydrated sulfate-bearing layers, PIA21935. https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mixtures-of-sulfates-in-melas-chasma/","[10] NASA Planetary Data System, Geosciences Node, 2026. “MRO: CRISM.” Hyperspectral archive for mineralogical mapping in Valles Marineris. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/crism.htm","[11] NASA Planetary Data System, Geosciences Node, 2026. “Mars Global Surveyor: MOLA.” Altimetry and topographic map archive. https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/mola.html"],"quickLinks":[{"label":"MRO CRISM archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/crism.htm"},{"label":"MRO mission / PDS entry point","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mro/"},{"label":"MOLA topography archive","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/mola.html"},{"label":"MOLA MEGDR topography","url":"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/mgs/megdr.html"},{"label":"NASA HiRISE/CRISM sulfate example","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mixtures-of-sulfates-in-melas-chasma/"},{"label":"NASA HiRISE layered sediments","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/layered-sediments-in-valles-marineris-2/"},{"label":"USGS geologic map","url":"https://doi.org/10.3133/i2010"}],"kicker":"FIELD NOTE / 04","query":"Valles Marineris Mars canyon NASA","localImage":null}};
const key=new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('site')||'jezero';
const s=sites[key]||sites.jezero;
const esc=t=>String(t).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
const linkify=t=>{
  const safe=esc(t);
  return safe.replace(/https?:\/\/[^\s<]+/g,m=>{
    const clean=m.replace(/[.,;)]+$/,'');
    const tail=m.slice(clean.length);
    return `<a href="${clean}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${clean}</a>${tail}`;
  });
};
const slug=t=>t.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g,'-').replace(/(^-|-$)/g,'');

document.title=`${s.title} — Tour de Mars`;
document.getElementById('detailKicker').textContent=s.kicker;
document.getElementById('detailName').textContent=s.title;
document.getElementById('detailIntro').textContent=s.subtitle;
document.getElementById('detailHeading').textContent=s.subtitle;
document.getElementById('detailBasis').textContent=s.basis;

document.getElementById('detailChips').innerHTML=s.meta.slice(1).map(x=>`<span class="chip">${esc(x.value)}</span>`).join('');
document.getElementById('detailMeta').innerHTML=s.meta.map(x=>`<div class="field-meta-card"><small>${esc(x.label)}</small><b>${esc(x.value)}</b></div>`).join('');

document.getElementById('detailToc').innerHTML=s.sections.map(sec=>`<a href="#${slug(sec.title)}">${esc(sec.title.split('.')[0])}</a>`).join('')+`<a href="#references">R</a>`;

document.getElementById('detailBody').innerHTML=s.sections.map(sec=>{
  let html=`<section class="field-note-section" id="${slug(sec.title)}"><h3>${esc(sec.title)}</h3>`;
  let inList=false;
  for(const item of sec.items){
    if(item.type==='bullet'){if(!inList){html+='<ul>';inList=true}html+=`<li>${linkify(item.text)}</li>`}
    else{if(inList){html+='</ul>';inList=false}html+=`<p>${linkify(item.text)}</p>`}
  }
  if(inList)html+='</ul>';
  return html+'</section>';
}).join('')+`<section class="field-note-section" id="references"><h3>References</h3><div class="reference-list">${s.references.map(r=>`<div class="reference-item">${linkify(r)}</div>`).join('')}</div></section>`;

const projectLinks=key==='jezero'?[{label:'Open live Jezero map',url:'live-map.html'},{label:'Enter MarsWalk',url:'marswalk.html'}]:[{label:'Open NASA Mars map',url:'live-map.html'},{label:'Visit MarsWalk prototype',url:'marswalk.html'}];
const original=(s.quickLinks||[]).slice(0,5);
document.getElementById('detailSourceLinks').innerHTML=[...projectLinks,...original].map(x=>{
  const external=/^https?:/.test(x.url);
  return `<a href="${esc(x.url)}"${external?' target="_blank" rel="noopener"':''}>${esc(x.label)} ↗</a>`;
}).join('');

const photo=document.getElementById('detailPhoto');
photo.style.backgroundImage='radial-gradient(circle at 65% 35%,#b77c5d,#5a3d38 46%,#161b1f)';
if(s.localImage){
  photo.style.backgroundImage=`url("${s.localImage}")`;
  photo.style.backgroundPosition='center 53%';
  photo.removeAttribute('data-query');
}else{
  photo.dataset.query=s.query;
  (async()=>{try{const r=await fetch('https://images-api.nasa.gov/search?q='+encodeURIComponent(s.query)+'&media_type=image&page_size=10');const j=await r.json();const item=(j.collection?.items||[]).find(x=>x.links?.length);const img=item?.links?.find(x=>x.render==='image')?.href||item?.links?.[0]?.href;if(img)photo.style.backgroundImage=`url("${img}")`}catch(e){}})();
}
